course: dictionary
lesson: 479
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Read the context to find out which meaning the writer wants. «Each squeeze sends a wave along your arteries.» The squeezing and the wave are about blood moving in your body, so artery must mean a tube in the body.
explain: An artery is a tube in the body that carries blood away from the heart to the rest of the body. «The pulse is the beat of the heart felt where an artery is near the skin.» You can feel it at your wrist or neck.
explain: Writers also use a word in a new way, like a picture. A main road or river that carries lots of traffic is sometimes called an artery. The word is not about blood now. It means a busy route that carries many things along. To check, swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
fact: An artery is a tube in the body that carries blood away from the heart.
fact: «The heart pumps blood out through arteries, into tiny capillaries and back through veins.»
fact: You can feel your pulse in an artery at your wrist or neck.
fact: «Artery» can also mean a main road or river that carries lots of traffic.
fact: In first aid, bleeding from an artery is very serious.
exercise: «Each squeeze sends a wave along your arteries.» What is an artery in this sentence? | *A tube in the body that carries blood | A dark cave under the ground | A kind of bird | A road full of cars
exercise: The heart pumps blood out through arteries. Which way does an artery carry blood? | Down into the ground | Nowhere, it stays still | Back to the heart, into the heart again | *Away from the heart
exercise: «The new motorway was the main artery into the city, full of lorries.» What does artery mean here? | A pond | *A main road that carries lots of traffic | A tube in the body | A small bone
test: «The pulse is the beat of the heart felt where an artery is near the skin.» Where can you feel a pulse? | In your hair | Inside your shoes | At your knees only | *At your wrist or neck
test: Ayesha says the river was the city's artery. What does she mean? | *The river was a main route that carried lots of boats and people | The river had a blocked tube in it | The river was very old | The river was a kind of fish
exam: Why does artery mean something different in «the main artery into town»? | *Because it is a new meaning, a busy route, not a tube in the body | Because the writer made a spelling mistake | Because the word means blood in every sentence | Because arteries are only found in towns
